    Heading("Rows").h1
    if FEATURES.billing { Link("Billing", to: "/billing") }
    match rows {
        loading { Spinner }
        error(e) { Text(e.message).danger }
        ready(r) { Text("{r.length} rows") }
    }
}

page Billing(path: "/billing", title: "Billing", description: "Plans and invoices.") {
    Heading("Billing").h1
}
"#, "webfluent.app.json": #"{
  "name": "environments-and-secrets"
}
"# }) {
        code {
            Markdown("**Needs:** an API that answers `/api/rows`, and the values set as [Set a value](#set-a-value) shows. It claims `/`: paste it into a new project, or replace the starter's `src/pages/Home.wf` with it.")
            CodeBlock(#"const API = env.PUBLIC_API ?? "/api"
const FEATURES = { billing: env.PUBLIC_BILLING == "on" }

page Rows(path: "/", title: "Rows", description: "Rows from the API.") {
    resource rows = fetch("{API}/rows")
    Heading("Rows").h1
    if FEATURES.billing { Link("Billing", to: "/billing") }
    match rows {
        loading { Spinner }
        error(e) { Text(e.message).danger }
        ready(r) { Text("{r.length} rows") }
    }
}

page Billing(path: "/billing", title: "Billing", description: "Plans and invoices.") {
    Heading("Billing").h1
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("`env.PUBLIC_API` is the value of `PUBLIC_API`, fixed when the site is built. A name that nothing sets reads as nothing (`null`), so `??` supplies the default. Keep the values in `const`s at the top of the file, as above, so every page reads them from one place.\n\nThe sections below set the values, keep the private ones out of the bundle, and build once per environment.")

        Heading("Set a value", id: "set-a-value").h2

        Markdown("You can set a value in three places. When two set the same name, the later one in this list wins:\n\n1. **The config's `env` map**, committed with the project:\n\n   ```json \{ \"env\": \{ \"PUBLIC_API\": \"/api\", \"PUBLIC_BILLING\": \"off\" \} \} ```\n\n2. **A `.env` file** beside `webfluent.app.json`, which you do not commit. `wf init` adds `.env` to `.gitignore`. Each line is `NAME=value`. A line that starts with `#` is a comment, a line may start with `export `, and a value may be in single or double quotes:\n\n   ```bash PUBLIC_API=http://localhost:8080/api PUBLIC_BILLING=on ```\n\n3. **The shell** that runs the build, for CI and one-off builds:\n\n   ```bash PUBLIC_API=https://api.example.com wf build ```\n\n   The shell supplies a name that begins `PUBLIC_`, or a private name the config or `.env` already declares. It does not bring in every variable the build happens to run with, such as `HOME`.\n\n   A name that `public_env` makes public without the prefix takes its value from the config or `.env` only — `public_env` is part of the project, and a project you cloned could list `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` there to publish your shell's value. To let the shell supply one, name it in `WF_ALLOW_SHELL_ENV`, comma-separated:\n\n   ```bash WF_ALLOW_SHELL_ENV=ANALYTICS_ID ANALYTICS_ID=UA-2 wf build ```")

        Callout("Versions before 4.1 read only the config's `env` map.", kind: .note)

        Heading("Keep a private value out of the bundle", id: "keep-a-private-value-out-of-the-bundle").h2

        Markdown("The build replaces `env.NAME` with its value in the JavaScript and HTML that every reader downloads. That is right for an API address and wrong for an API key. So a page, a component, a store or an `api` block may read only a name that says it is public:\n\n- a name that begins with **`PUBLIC_`**, or\n- a name listed in the config's **`public_env`**.\n\nIn this config, `env.PUBLIC_API` and `env.ANALYTICS_ID` are public, and `env.STRIPE_SECRET` is private:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "env": { "PUBLIC_API": "/api/v1", "ANALYTICS_ID": "UA-1", "STRIPE_SECRET": "sk_…" },
  "public_env": ["ANALYTICS_ID"]
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A page may read `env.PUBLIC_API` and `env.ANALYTICS_ID`. Reading `env.STRIPE_SECRET` from a page is a compile error ([`E108`](/errors/E108)) that names the file and the line.\n\nA private value is still available to [server rendering](/recipes/render-a-template), which runs on a server, where a secret stays secret. It never reaches the bundle: the build writes only public values into it.")

        Callout("Adding a name to `public_env` publishes its value to every reader of the site, and every build says so with a warning naming it. A secret that a third party needs belongs in a request your server makes, not in a page ([Security](/about/security#secrets-stay-out-of-the-bundle)).", kind: .caution)

        Heading("Name your own values something other than env", id: "name-your-own-values-something-other-than-env").h2

        Markdown("`env` is the language's name for these settings, and nothing else may use it. A `state`, a prop, a route parameter, a loop item or a top-level `const` called `env` is an error ([`E118`](/errors/E118)). For a value that says which stage a page is about, use a name such as `target` or `stage`.")

        Heading("Build once per environment", id: "build-once-per-environment").h2

        Markdown("To deploy the same source to several environments, run one build per environment, each with its own values:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build                                              # .env on your machine"),
                Line(command: "PUBLIC_API=https://staging.example.com/api wf build   # staging"),
                Line(command: "PUBLIC_API=https://api.example.com wf build           # production")
            ])

        Markdown("In CI, set the variables on the job's build step:")

        CodeBlock(#"- run: ~/.webfluent/bin/wf build
  env:
    PUBLIC_API: https://api.example.com
    PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_ID: ${{ vars.ANALYTICS_ID }}"#, title: "yaml")

        Markdown("Each build overwrites `build/`, so deploy what one build wrote before running the next, each to its own host ([Ship](/recipes#ship)).")

        Heading("Change other settings per deploy", id: "change-other-settings-per-deploy").h2

        Markdown("A few settings outside `env` also change between deploys:")

        Table(caption: "Change other settings per deploy") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Setting").header
                    Table.Cell("What it is").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`build.base_path`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The sub-path the site is served under. The build prefixes every link, asset and route with it ([Ship](/recipes#ship))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`meta.site_url`") }
                    Table.Cell("The site's absolute address, for canonical links, the sitemap and sharing tags")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme.tokens`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Design tokens a pipeline supplies, on top of the theme: `\{ \"theme\": \{ \"tokens\": \{ \"color-primary\": \"#8B5CF6\" \} \} \}`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("These settings live in `webfluent.app.json`. There is no separate config file per environment. A pipeline that needs different settings writes the file before it builds; `wf config set` edits it from a script:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf config set build.base_path /staging")
            ])

        Heading("Audit what the project reads", id: "audit-what-the-project-reads").h2

        Markdown("To list every `env` name the project has, run `wf audit`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf audit")
            ])

        Markdown("It prints each name, where it is read, and whether it is public, beside everything else the site trusts ([Review a site's security before you deploy](/recipes/review-security)). Run it before a release, and when you review a change that adds a name.")

    }
}
